    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-8">Our systemic approach</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">In family therapy the focus is not a single individual but the system of relationships. A family member's behaviour takes on its meaning alongside everyone else's.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">Think of a child who won't go to school in the mornings. Treat it only as the child's anxiety and you miss how those mornings go at home: who raises their voice, who steps in, how the argument stops the moment the child starts getting ready. School refusal sometimes works to hold the tension in the house in one place.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-8">This view blames no one. It replaces &ldquo;who started this&rdquo; with &ldquo;how does this loop keep turning&rdquo; — a question everyone in the room can answer, where the first one has no answer at all.</p>
        <div class="bg-primary/5 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-primary/15">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm mb-2">How long does it take?</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Family therapy tends to be shorter-term than individual therapy, often 6–12 sessions. Some families come back for periodic sessions as needs arise.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-warm">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-8">What happens in a family session</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="flex gap-5">
            <div class="flex-shrink-0"><div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-2xl bg-primary text-white flex items-center justify-center font-semibold text-lg">1</div></div>
            <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary flex-1">
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">We decide together who attends</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Sometimes the whole family, sometimes the parents on their own, sometimes one parent and one child. Not everyone has to be in the room every time. In the first session we work out who it would be useful to include.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex gap-5">
            <div class="flex-shrink-0"><div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-2xl bg-primary text-white flex items-center justify-center font-semibold text-lg">2</div></div>
            <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary flex-1">
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">Every account gets room</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">The same event looks different in each member's telling, and each version is valid. The psychologist does not take sides; deciding who is right is not our job. Our job is to make a space where every voice in the room can be heard.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex gap-5">
            <div class="flex-shrink-0"><div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-2xl bg-primary text-white flex items-center justify-center font-semibold text-lg">3</div></div>
            <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary flex-1">
              <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink mb-2">We work on the repeating pattern</h3>
              <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Which sentence starts the argument, who withdraws, who steps in, how it closes. Once that sequence is visible, each member can see where their own part could change. That is usually where movement begins.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-8">Two things families often ask</h2>
        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-serif text-lg text-ink mb-3">&ldquo;One of us won't come&rdquo;</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">This happens often. The reluctance usually comes from fear — of being blamed, or of it turning out to be pointless. We don't try to talk that person into attending. Starting with the members who do come already changes something in the loop, and others sometimes join in later weeks.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-warm rounded-2xl p-6 border border-warm-tertiary">
            <h3 class="font-serif text-lg text-ink mb-3">&ldquo;Is what I say on my own passed on?&rdquo;</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Family therapy sometimes includes separate sessions with individual members. How anything raised there will be handled with the family is agreed in advance. Sessions and your personal information are protected in line with our confidentiality principles.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-warm">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">When generations live side by side</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">In Cyprus families often live close to one another. Grandparents are part of daily care, relatives live on the same street, and decisions are rarely made without consulting the older generation. That closeness carries a family; it is also where boundaries blur.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">A young couple building their own household carries the expectations of two families at once. Parents want to raise children by the rules they grew up with, while those children are growing up inside a different generation. The conflict usually comes not from ill will but from two versions of what is right, both trying to hold at the same time.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">The aim in these sessions is not to move anyone away from their family. We look together at where a boundary can be set without breaking the relationship, who has a say in which decision, and how expectations can be spoken about.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="py-16 bg-white">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-5 lg:px-8">
        <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl text-ink mb-6">Family therapy, or another route?</h2>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed mb-4">If the difficulty circulates inside a relationship, family therapy is a reasonable place to start — but not every situation has to be handled in the family room. When it sits mainly between partners, couples sessions may fit better; when it is a weight one person is carrying, <a href="/en/services/individual-therapy.html" class="text-primary hover:underline">individual therapy</a> may be the more useful route.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-muted leading-relaxed">Depending on your child's age, <a href="/en/services/child-psychology.html" class="text-primary hover:underline">child psychology</a> or <a href="/en/services/adolescent-psychology.html" class="text-primary hover:underline">adolescent psychology</a> sessions can run alongside the family work. For relatives who live in another city or country, <a href="/en/services/online-therapy.html" class="text-primary hover:underline">online sessions</a> are an option. We consider which route suits your situation together in the first session.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
